[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234045 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f39fa11c-0222-54e9-8d62-4eb053a5813d" :question/math-academy-id "q-43381" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\operatorname{cos}{165}^{∘}.$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${165}^{∘}={120}^{∘}+{45}^{∘}.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $x={120}^{∘}$ and $y={45}^{∘}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}({120}^{∘}+{45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}{120}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}{45}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}{120}^{∘}\\operatorname{sin}{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234046 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "11214dee-7d32-5b34-bc7e-86bbedbaab36" :question/math-academy-id "q-43384" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{cos}v+\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x-\\operatorname{cos}x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234047 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "099149c9-1e5d-5a49-aba3-6528a838f44d" :question/math-academy-id "q-43462" :question/problem "Express $6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right)$ as a single trigonometric ratio." :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{10}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right) & =\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{10})=\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right)=3\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right)=3\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234066 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "29aabb6a-6d0f-575f-b3d9-7aa494e00fc4" :question/math-academy-id "q-46975" :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}$ and $q(x)=\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},$ what is ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we divide the two functions, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{4}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the algebra of limits to pull out the constant factor of $2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\right) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\infty }(2\\cdot \\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)=0,\n$$\n\nWe can evaluate our limit as follows:\n\n$$\n2\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}\\right)=2\\cdot 0=0\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234098 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a89ce7a-fa47-59a8-9415-c78aa2d53e4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,495$ computers per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186327306} {:answer/id #uuid "803a4afa-231e-5fe3-9620-a3bb07d38e89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,501$ computers per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328039} {:answer/id #uuid "9d60b585-4fa9-5f20-8f3a-204234d31c77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,695$ computers per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328295} {:answer/id #uuid "cb920988-1780-5aa1-8056-120bf2b80e61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,699$ computers per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328717} {:answer/id #uuid "cd96f054-bbd5-52aa-84e8-cccbee30421b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,801$ computers per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cb920988-1780-5aa1-8056-120bf2b80e61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$1\\,699$ computers per day" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328717} :answer-field/id #uuid "deabcc85-8343-5de1-b482-39dd8cf37dd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186329573}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ffff29ad-d952-5fbe-a5da-7e7612a4d92a" :question/math-academy-id "q-48700" :question/problem "The number of computers affected by the spread of some ransomware after $t$ days can be modeled by the function $f(t)=\\frac{100\\,({t}^{3}+2)}{t+1}.$ What is the instantaneous rate of change in the number of infected computers when $t=9$ days?" :question/worked-solution "The instantaneous rate of change in the spread when $t=9$ days is equal to ${f}^{′}(9).$ \n\nFirst, we take the derivative and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{100\\,({t}^{3}+2)}{t+1}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{100(3{t}^{2})(t+1)-100({t}^{3}+2)(1)}{{(t+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{2{t}^{3}+3{t}^{2}-2}{{(t+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting $t=9,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(9) & =100\\cdot \\frac{2(9)^{3}+3(9)^{2}-2}{{((9)+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{1\\,458+243-2}{100} \\\\  & =1\\,699.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the instantaneous rate of change in the spread when $t=9$ is $1\\,699$ computers per day."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234106 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b6c1d13e-58bd-57d8-918b-0a1bebf09fb1" :question/math-academy-id "q-49173" :question/problem "$\\int {(1+5{x}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "Using the sum and constant factor rules, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {(1+5{x}^{2})}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (1+10{x}^{2}+25{x}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =25\\int {x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x+10\\int {x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =25\\left(\\frac{{x}^{5}}{5}\\right)+10\\left(\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}\\right)+(x)+C \\\\  & =5{x}^{5}+\\frac{10{x}^{3}}{3}+x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234112 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b1e3f33e-bdbb-5cde-8a13-77a7fa24e262" :question/math-academy-id "q-49558" :question/problem "What is the fifth partial sum of the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }6{x}^{n+1}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=6{x}^{n+1}.$ We note that this is a geometric sequence with $r=x$ and first term given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=6{x}^{1+1}=6{x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe $k$th partial sum of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{s}_{k}={a}_{1}\\left(\\frac{1-{r}^{k}}{1-r}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fifth partial sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{5} & =6{x}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1-{x}^{5}}{1-x} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{x}^{2}(1-{x}^{5})}{1-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234114 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7c8f233c-e3cc-5e35-bfda-201f5d20a8c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-49635" :question/problem "What is the fourth partial sum of the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }4(2x)^{n}?$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}=4(2x)^{n}.$ We note that this is a geometric sequence with $r=2x$ and first term given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=4(2x)^{1}=4\\cdot 2x=8x.\n$$\n\nThe $k$th partial sum of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{s}_{k}={a}_{1}\\left(\\frac{1-{r}^{k}}{1-r}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth partial sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{4} & =8x\\cdot \\frac{1-(2x)^{4}}{1-2x} \\\\  & =8x\\cdot \\frac{1-{2}^{4}{x}^{4}}{1-2x} \\\\  & =8x\\cdot \\frac{1-16{x}^{4}}{1-2x} \\\\  & =\\frac{8x(1-16{x}^{4})}{1-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234142 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "44ff8394-c8e2-5860-8296-306905059970" :question/math-academy-id "q-53754" :question/problem "The equation of a curve is $3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}=9.$ The normal line at the point $(3,\\,3)$ intersects the $y$-axis at the point $P.$ What are the coordinates of $P?$" :question/worked-solution "First of all, we need to find the slope of the normal line at the point $(3,\\,3).$  Remember that the slope of the normal line, ${m}^{'},$ is the negative reciprocal of the slope of the tangent line:\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we will calculate the derivative using implicit differentiation, assuming that $y=y(x).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}-2{y}^{2}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(9) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2})-\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{y}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ 6x-4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ -4y\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-6x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{-6x}{-4y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{3x}{2y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the slope of the tangent at the point $(3,\\,3)$ is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & ={\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}_{(3,\\,3)} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3x}{2y}\\right)}_{(3,\\,3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\cdot 3}{2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is ${m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3/2}=-\\frac{2}{3}.$ We can write the equation of the normal at the point $(3,\\,3)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(y-{y}_{1}) & ={m}^{'}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ (y-3) & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-3) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-3)+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we just need to find where this normal line intersects the $y$-axis. At the point $P$ the $x$-coordinate is $0,$ so we set $x=0$ and solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{2}{3}(x-3)+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}(0-3)+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2}{3}(-3)+3 \\\\ y & =2+3 \\\\ y & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of $P$ are $(0,\\,5).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234163 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "106793da-24f7-59ed-8b62-e13cfc57d52f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512133} {:answer/id #uuid "571aa39e-400a-5863-b676-0015f3b249cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512297} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1bcd9e-18d9-5b81-bf60-b688fcd821e3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512361} {:answer/id #uuid "6fa661ae-e5a1-55f6-8a14-031daabbb4f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512364} {:answer/id #uuid "a7e88750-2d2a-5ed0-bb69-82c918766691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512498}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7e88750-2d2a-5ed0-bb69-82c918766691" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512498} :answer-field/id #uuid "981ea52b-9e45-5858-a199-002953f70e37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512804}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "727e7006-93ff-5e5c-a29e-52adc22e5960" :question/math-academy-id "q-64010" :question/problem "The third term of a geometric sequence is $108$ and its fifth term is $3.$  Find the common ratio $r$ of this sequence given that $r$ is positive." :question/worked-solution "To find the common ratio $r$ of a geometric sequence given two terms ${a}_{m}$ and ${a}_{n},$ we use the formula\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're given ${a}_{5}=3$ and ${a}_{3}=108.$ Substituting $n=5$ and $m=3$ into the formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{5-3} & =\\frac{{a}_{5}}{{a}_{3}} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{108} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, solving the equation for $r$ and considering that $r$ is positive, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{36} \\\\ {r}^{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{6}\\right)}^{2} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234168 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04b1019a-d92e-5d04-be9a-1a4844c6f0a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512114} {:answer/id #uuid "706750fb-a8db-5883-9866-c48f23ec7c80" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512367} {:answer/id #uuid "767844f9-50f9-5d37-92c5-befa41f424fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512379} {:answer/id #uuid "82a50b38-647a-51f0-83dd-2e735fd413fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512404} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf7300d-5c9b-5c16-914a-b75e51c21227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512673}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ecf7300d-5c9b-5c16-914a-b75e51c21227" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512673} :answer-field/id #uuid "427c3f91-22fc-5c34-bf37-633a78d7c0ba" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7c210cee-fd55-5399-a645-1646a3c5f4b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-64741" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{10}\\right)$ in terms of a reference angle." :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{10}\\right)\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{180}^{∘}}{\\pi }\\right)={126}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\frac{7\\pi }{10}$ is equivalent to ${126}^{∘},$ which lies in the $2\\text{nd}$ quadrant.\n\n![](images/89/89b5e2656378adb5d91c9f58c261e4beaae252b67819b904bf209f6ed8a5a25e.png)\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{10}\\right)$ in terms of $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we follow three steps:\n\n1\\. Find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n2\\. Calculate the value of the function for ${\\theta }_{R}.$\n\n3\\. Determine whether the resulting value is positive or negative.\n\n**Step 1**: Since $\\theta =\\frac{7\\pi }{10}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\pi -\\theta  \\\\  & =\\pi -\\frac{7\\pi }{10} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: The given ratio is $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{10}\\right)$, and therefore we're interested in $\\operatorname{cot}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right).$\n\n**Step 3:** The ratio $\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{10}\\right)$ must be negative because tangent (and therefore cotangent) is always negative in the $2$nd quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{10}\\right)=-\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}\\right).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234172 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b768fa0-11ab-5e73-8e7b-94e2006f5adc" :question/math-academy-id "q-64921" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234173 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e7de71bc-9713-56d3-bb2c-382ec6136954" :question/math-academy-id "q-64923" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234174 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "52fdb8a8-7b9e-5f48-95b6-5c145626cfac" :question/math-academy-id "q-64929" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/a3/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234175 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a127de87-14ab-55a4-9799-daf596514c1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-64934" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cot}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234176 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0bcc7bdb-2729-5c47-b53f-cc1a0afb9442" :question/math-academy-id "q-64941" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{tan}\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{11\\pi }{6}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234177 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c9b162b5-76a3-5c84-890b-7a8a4d9ae977" :question/math-academy-id "q-64949" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{csc}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)}=-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{2}}=-\\sqrt{2}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234178 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1914e519-4beb-5bb8-9a38-50ed69e1d866" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333216} {:answer/id #uuid "22a3763d-dc3d-5935-ae88-e9d3b27f8f29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333304} {:answer/id #uuid "b155a025-a94e-5977-b956-685f68f80110" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334546} {:answer/id #uuid "b6cfb70b-94dc-5a36-8f8e-81681c40c155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334589} {:answer/id #uuid "def47a80-2f7e-56c3-8be2-77ec664ed8cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b6cfb70b-94dc-5a36-8f8e-81681c40c155" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334589} :answer-field/id #uuid "007f7b97-0377-5168-b14c-df88cbf2c3f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335239}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8f2cac98-d46c-5a68-832a-abb863407c09" :question/math-academy-id "q-65502" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234179 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f08e504a-4e7b-5af5-bce4-4991a275c783" :question/math-academy-id "q-65551" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{13\\pi }{12}\\right).$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{13\\pi }{12}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x-y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{13\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234180 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9b896832-365a-53e2-87c9-f14f4fb24d89" :question/math-academy-id "q-65556" :question/problem "Find the exact value of  $\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right).$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$*" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x-y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{12}\\right) & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right)+\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234181 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "effa2204-3ea7-5fd6-aed4-18aa87f24c7a" :question/math-academy-id "q-65608" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)}{{sin}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}2x=2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})=2\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)=2\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)}{{sin}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}{{sin}^{2}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cot}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234203 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96bd64a4-a693-5801-a289-d7188f556814" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)=-100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520204} {:answer/id #uuid "ae3500ce-45b9-5f87-93da-ff417d20739d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡(-2)=\\frac{1}{100}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520232} {:answer/id #uuid "b9ac4ee7-f1da-55c7-b937-45bb16de187b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520251} {:answer/id #uuid "d197b142-08f6-5694-bdaa-5a735426ca9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡100=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520284} {:answer/id #uuid "f635d2ff-c815-5271-95f7-80002177a122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f635d2ff-c815-5271-95f7-80002177a122" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)=-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520351} :answer-field/id #uuid "04a0273e-3e34-5845-8894-910743f99cff" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186520367}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f9714aa-ab20-5f59-96d2-702c693f996b" :question/math-academy-id "q-66543" :question/problem "${10}^{-2}=\\frac{1}{100}$ can be expressed as" :question/worked-solution "The common logarithm of $a$ is defined as follows:\n\n$a={10}^{x}$ is equivalent to $\\operatorname{log}⁡a=x$\n\nIf we compare ${10}^{-2}=\\frac{1}{100}$ with ${10}^{x}=a,$ we get that $a=\\frac{1}{100}$ and $x=-2.$\n\nTherefore, the given equation can be expressed as $\\operatorname{log}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{100}\\right)=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28ec0d1a-fbc1-5041-8e40-636cd7efa33f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542747} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc1e664-6c62-5c39-b7eb-8b0cdd0da386" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542848} {:answer/id #uuid "4ecfee0c-ab43-511e-bbf7-24b4470eee72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542854} {:answer/id #uuid "bf0a2323-dacf-55d7-8bd0-de0f8bb99950" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543142} {:answer/id #uuid "f507a8d2-4685-5d46-9b8b-f4be0c73f455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543266}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28ec0d1a-fbc1-5041-8e40-636cd7efa33f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542747} :answer-field/id #uuid "51e26732-12ca-5a69-9f16-67129aa50da4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186543341}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f70da637-39bf-585b-b7da-8cee169442e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-66772" :question/problem "$6{log}_{5}⁡(100)-6{log}_{5}⁡(4)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the quotient rule for logarithms:\n\n$$\n{log}_{n}⁡(a)-{log}_{n}⁡(b)={log}_{n}⁡\\left(\\frac{a}{b}\\right)\n$$\n\nIf we factor the given expression and use the quotient rule for logarithms, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{log}_{5}⁡(100)-6{log}_{5}⁡(4) & =6({log}_{5}⁡(100)-{log}_{5}⁡(4)) \\\\  & =6{log}_{5}⁡\\left(\\frac{100}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =6{log}_{5}⁡(25)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since ${5}^{2}=25$, then ${log}_{5}⁡(25)={log}_{5}⁡({5}^{2})=2.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n6{log}_{5}⁡(25)=6\\cdot 2=12.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234214 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "21790270-f0c6-579c-bc00-b6d3f7c66abb" :question/math-academy-id "q-66880" :question/problem "${log}_{25}(\\sqrt{125})=$" :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=25$ and $X=\\sqrt{125}.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ can be presented as a power of $5,$ we set $b=5.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{25}(\\sqrt{125}) & =\\frac{{log}_{5}(\\sqrt{125})}{{log}_{5}(25)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{5}(\\sqrt{{5}^{3}})}{{log}_{5}({5}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{5}({5}^{3/2})}{{log}_{5}({5}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234215 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "6da34c64-2d10-5dfd-84fd-99833321fbb7" :question/math-academy-id "q-66887" :question/problem "${log}_{9}(\\sqrt{27})=$" :question/worked-solution "We use the change of base formula:\n\n$$\n{log}_{a}X=\\frac{{log}_{b}X}{{log}_{b}a}\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a=9$ and $X=\\sqrt{27}.$ Since both $a$ and $X$ can be presented as a power of $3,$ we set $b=3.$ So, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{9}(\\sqrt{27}) & =\\frac{{log}_{3}(\\sqrt{27})}{{log}_{3}(9)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{3}(\\sqrt{{3}^{3}})}{{log}_{3}({3}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{log}_{3}({3}^{3/2})}{{log}_{3}({3}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234227 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e19ea6c4-dfeb-565f-a2a0-6ee2fc13d7bd" :question/math-academy-id "q-67217" :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{n+1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $0,$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nComputing the first few terms, we get:\n\n$$\n{a}_{0}={\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{0+1}=\\frac{3}{4}\n$$\n\n \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{1+1}={\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the common ratio is \n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{{a}_{0}}=\\frac{{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{2}}{\\frac{3}{4}}=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=0}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}^{n+1} & =\\frac{{a}_{0}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}{1-\\frac{3}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}\\cdot \\frac{4}{1} \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234228 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4418018-1aed-5319-a755-8b808b68a382" :question/math-academy-id "q-67275" :question/problem "Consider the positive infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $100$ and a third term equal to $4.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=100$ and ${a}_{3}=4.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3-1} & =\\frac{4}{100} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{25} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{25}} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have taken the positive square root since we're told that the sequence is positive.\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{(1-\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{100}{\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =100\\cdot \\frac{5}{4} \\\\  & =125.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234229 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f9956293-574c-5417-9cec-a06683f0f54b" :question/math-academy-id "q-67277" :question/problem "Consider the infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $36$ and a fourth term equal to $\\frac{4}{3}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=36$ and ${a}_{4}=\\frac{4}{3}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{4-1} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{4}{3}\\right)}{36} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{27} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{36}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =54.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234230 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "08a0745f-afdb-5fd5-896c-aecc342af1a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538254} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8016a8-51b3-5faa-84af-ae76ac8a14e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "107" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538511} {:answer/id #uuid "8b7e6727-bee6-581b-b90f-15564547ab7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538557} {:answer/id #uuid "c7ae6245-2f0d-51cd-8955-540a0d399231" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "98" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538698} {:answer/id #uuid "f5bc4753-d8a5-5d2c-9d61-78c209c31473" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "81" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b7e6727-bee6-581b-b90f-15564547ab7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538557} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed996d10-640b-5a4b-971a-4d70ea88d425" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186538914}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e60b8545-b8cf-54c2-98e5-563869147ce1" :question/math-academy-id "q-67278" :question/problem "Consider the infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $48$ and a fourth term equal to $\\frac{3}{4}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=48$ and ${a}_{4}=\\frac{3}{4}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{4-1} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)}{48} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{(1-\\frac{1}{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =48\\cdot \\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234231 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b6e779a9-2c4f-5235-b281-a8e7c8502319" :question/math-academy-id "q-67279" :question/problem "Consider the infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $35$ and a fourth term equal to $-\\frac{56}{25}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=35$ and ${a}_{4}=-\\frac{56}{25}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{4-1} & =\\frac{\\left(-\\frac{56}{25}\\right)}{35} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =-\\frac{8}{125} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{3} \\\\ r & =-\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{(1-\\left(-\\frac{2}{5}\\right))} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{(1+\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{35}{\\left(\\frac{7}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =35\\cdot \\frac{5}{7} \\\\  & =25.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234233 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9e8ff1e-8d9a-5457-b007-b162540fa5bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-67304" :question/problem "Evaluate the series ${\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the index $n$ starts from $2.$ However, it is still a geometric series because it is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n}.$ \n\nIn this case, the first term is ${a}_{2},$ and so the sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of this geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1.\n$$\n\nFor the first few terms, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} & ={\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{2}=\\frac{4}{25} \\\\ {a}_{3} & ={\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{3}=\\frac{8}{125}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{3}}{{a}_{2}}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{8}{125}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{4}{25}\\right)}=\\frac{2}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the sum of the series is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=2}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{2}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{4}{25}\\right)}{(1-\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{4}{25}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234234 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bde260c1-aa27-51ed-b795-2836a2c1b1cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-67324" :question/problem "The geometric series $-2+12-72+432$ can be expressed using sigma notation as" :question/worked-solution "We must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=-2$ and $N=4.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we calculate the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{12}{-2}=-6\n$$\n\nTherefore, expressing the given geometric series in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2+12-72+432 & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}(-2)\\cdot (-6)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify the above using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}(-2)\\cdot (-6)^{n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}(-2)\\cdot (-6)^{n}\\cdot (-6)^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}(-2)\\cdot {(-6)}^{-1}\\cdot (-6)^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}(-2)\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{6}\\right)(-6)^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-6)^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234236 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6ebb388a-1d96-5595-b941-783f48138ff2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}\\frac{1}{6}(-5)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375276} {:answer/id #uuid "cf9fa20e-44c9-5446-972c-00c8f8af69ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}6(-5)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375277} {:answer/id #uuid "da549f0d-5aec-59c7-b8f9-4fa7ad81a185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}\\left(-\\frac{5}{6}\\right){6}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375278} {:answer/id #uuid "5c9d79eb-0fe5-5458-bef3-98953d4b0a34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-30){6}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375279} {:answer/id #uuid "b8574742-20e4-52eb-b708-78b4238d95bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-5){6}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375280}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da549f0d-5aec-59c7-b8f9-4fa7ad81a185" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}\\left(-\\frac{5}{6}\\right){6}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375278} :answer-field/id #uuid "8466c624-6990-5b4b-a494-32bad67ce365" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375275}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f53b20a4-59af-597c-a9ed-4eff01857435" :question/math-academy-id "q-67377" :question/problem "The first term of a geometric sequence is $-5$ and the common ratio is $6.$ Written in sigma notation, what is the sum of the first $22$ terms?" :question/worked-solution "First, we must express the series in the form:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=-5,$ $r=6$ and $N=22.$\n\nExpressing the geometric series in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{S}_{22}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-5)\\cdot {6}^{n-1}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify the above using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-5)\\cdot {6}^{n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-5)\\cdot {6}^{n}\\cdot {6}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-5)\\cdot {6}^{-1}\\cdot {6}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot {6}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}\\left(-\\frac{5}{6}\\right){6}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234237 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f2ea1a6-10d2-55f9-9e1a-8b79ec9790f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570181} {:answer/id #uuid "2e253573-b142-5acf-8a8d-328d4bb8251e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570203} {:answer/id #uuid "9bf7b9c3-f8f3-5c91-8b55-0252e40b80c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570400} {:answer/id #uuid "af70ab77-7c59-59b2-95bf-8d3d88b0c70f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570442} {:answer/id #uuid "e2462a8d-9dc3-538f-b979-1fc867a6f9a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570519}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1f2ea1a6-10d2-55f9-9e1a-8b79ec9790f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{11}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570181} :answer-field/id #uuid "056c679c-2e61-54c8-b853-453b05e59436" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186570579}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aa75fc9d-104d-5e12-b331-4d8ff3973336" :question/math-academy-id "q-67439" :question/problem "Solve the equation $-\\frac{2}{x+3}=\\frac{8}{x-1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we note that\n\n- $x=-3$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the left-hand side zero\n\n- $x=1$ cannot be a solution since this would make the denominator on the right-hand side zero\n\nWe can flip an equation involving two fractions as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{c}{d}\\,⟹\\,\\frac{b}{a}=\\frac{d}{c}\n$$\n\nThe above technique works whenever $a$ and $c$ are both different from zero.\n\nTherefore, we can flip the given equation as follows:\n\n$$\n-\\frac{x+3}{2}=\\frac{x-1}{8}\n$$\n\nNext, we write both fractions using the least common denominator, $8.$ The equation becomes\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4x+12}{8}=\\frac{x-1}{8}.\n$$\n\nWe can now solve this equation with the addition and the multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-8)\\cdot \\frac{4x+12}{-8} & =(-8)\\cdot \\frac{x-1}{8} \\\\ 4x+12 & =-x+1 \\\\ 4x+x & =1-12 \\\\ 5x & =-11 \\\\ \\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\cdot (5x) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\cdot (-11) \\\\ (\\cancel{\\frac{1}{5}})\\cdot (\\cancel{5}x) & =\\left(\\frac{1}{5}\\right)\\cdot (-11) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{11}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234238 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "baa58408-c818-50ce-a7fd-9f3fca093860" :question/math-academy-id "q-67580" :question/problem "Given that $p(x)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}$ and $q(x)=-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3}}$, what is ${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's calculate ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x):$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{8}{{x}^{5}}\\right)=\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWhen we divide the two functions ${x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)$ and $q(x)$, we get a constant, and the limit of a constant is just the constant itself:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{{x}^{2}\\cdot p(x)}{q(x)} & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{\\left(\\frac{8}{{x}^{3}}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{2}{3{x}^{3}}\\right)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{8}{\\left(-\\frac{2}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\cdot (8) \\\\  & ={lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }-12 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234248 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "280e1cee-68f8-59f3-a023-f6dd1f086b41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333358} {:answer/id #uuid "c16fb7f6-a00e-595d-a28e-2f0a7397869c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334666} {:answer/id #uuid "d02e3a07-a9eb-5ee2-9205-057f65384e6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334807} {:answer/id #uuid "e4417143-3102-5cc2-b8d4-db87db4085b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334978} {:answer/id #uuid "eacbc418-3efa-5b4c-a585-26704419f165" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335043}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e4417143-3102-5cc2-b8d4-db87db4085b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334978} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b5f293a-3b57-563b-b236-87b7649c6cbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335256}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e3d07c4f-77fc-5e25-9502-ae72735f0f26" :question/math-academy-id "q-6832" :question/problem "For the parametric curve\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right),\\,\\,\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}\\right),\\,-3\\le t\\le 3,\n$$\n\nconsider the following table of values:\n\n| **$t$** | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $1$ | $0$ | $b$ | $0$ | $1$ | $0$ | $c$ |\n| $y$ | $a$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $1$ | $\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $-1$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\nFind the value of $-a+b+c.$" :question/worked-solution "- To find the value of $a,$ we substitute $t=-3$ into $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{3}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\na=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (-3)}{3}\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-\\pi )=-1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $b,$ we substitute $t=-1$ into $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\nb=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (-1)}{2}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)=-1.\n$$\n\n- To find the value of $c,$ we substitute $t=3$ into $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi t}{2}\\right).$ This gives\n\n$$\nc=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi (3)}{2}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\right)=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n-a+b+c=-(-1)+(-1)+(-1)=-1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234249 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "35e0bf9e-55a9-503e-bb55-0d8b6bfd2bca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{-3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552971} {:answer/id #uuid "38d4ba8e-ef5c-5be5-9602-4bfdc765be69" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6}^{3}\\cdot {7}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186552981} {:answer/id #uuid "87019656-9802-57c4-9c86-0aa7eee50d5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553139} {:answer/id #uuid "89435a2f-5b03-538d-abb8-6005a8a854de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6}^{3}\\cdot {7}^{-8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553142} {:answer/id #uuid "a1425a83-7601-5533-bdbe-983b40ba004e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a1425a83-7601-5533-bdbe-983b40ba004e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{6}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553193} :answer-field/id #uuid "f166422c-41b0-5717-a433-57648d5156df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186553481}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ce2bfa1c-5e37-5680-930e-c063dd6092a4" :question/math-academy-id "q-68957" :question/problem "${\\left(\\frac{6}{7}\\right)}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{3}=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the first factor using the power of quotient rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\left(\\frac{6}{7}\\right)}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{3}=\\frac{{6}^{8}}{{7}^{8}}\\cdot {7}^{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we rewrite the fractional part of our expression as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{6}^{8}}{{7}^{8}}\\cdot {7}^{3}={6}^{8}\\cdot \\frac{{7}^{3}}{{7}^{8}}\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify using the quotient rule for exponents:\n\n$$\n{6}^{8}\\cdot \\frac{{7}^{3}}{{7}^{8}}={6}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{3-8}={6}^{8}\\cdot {7}^{-5}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234277 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "57b9362b-dae2-5830-9256-de6f1a317b4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-70475" :question/problem "If $y=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}},$ find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$" :question/worked-solution "We first rewrite the function as\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{4}{\\sqrt{x}}=-4{x}^{-1/2}.\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the constant multiple and power rules:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4{x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{-1/2}) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right){x}^{(-\\frac{1}{2}-1)} \\\\  & =2{x}^{-3/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234283 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a3088bcb-433a-5990-b498-23accf3d3ce6" :question/math-academy-id "q-70739" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cot}\\theta +1}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cot}\\theta +1}=(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cot}\\theta +1)\n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identity $\\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ and we substitute it into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\operatorname{cot}\\theta +1) & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }+1) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we turn the division into multiplication, and simplify the expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )÷\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\right) & =(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\cdot \\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta )}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cot}\\theta +1}=\\operatorname{sin}\\theta .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234284 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9dd42d7-29a8-50a1-88a7-088fa3634660" :question/math-academy-id "q-70740" :question/problem "Simplify the trigonometric expression $\\frac{{cos}^{2}\\theta }{{cot}^{2}\\theta }.$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's interpret the fraction as a division:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{cos}^{2}\\theta }{{cot}^{2}\\theta }={cos}^{2}\\theta ÷{cot}^{2}\\theta \n$$\n\nThen, we recall the identity $\\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ and we substitute it into the above expression:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\theta ÷{cot}^{2}\\theta  & ={cos}^{2}\\theta ÷{\\left(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }\\right)}^{2} \\\\  & ={cos}^{2}\\theta ÷\\frac{{cos}^{2}\\theta }{{sin}^{2}\\theta }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we turn the division into multiplication, and simplify the expression as much as possible:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cos}^{2}\\theta ÷\\frac{{cos}^{2}\\theta }{{sin}^{2}\\theta } & ={cos}^{2}\\theta \\cdot \\frac{{sin}^{2}\\theta }{{cos}^{2}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}\\theta \\cdot {sin}^{2}\\theta }{{cos}^{2}\\theta } \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}\\theta }\\cdot {sin}^{2}\\theta }{\\cancel{{cos}^{2}\\theta }} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}\\theta }{1} \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{{cos}^{2}\\theta }{{cot}^{2}\\theta }={sin}^{2}\\theta .$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234298 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4c9da781-1702-5ac7-9948-600ab4559823" :question/math-academy-id "q-71096" :question/problem "Find the antiderivative of ${x}^{5/2}.$" :question/worked-solution "We can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{5}{2}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{5/2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{5/2+1}}{(\\frac{5}{2}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{7/2}}{\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, let's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{2{x}^{7/2}}{7}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{7}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{7/2})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{2}{7}\\cdot \\frac{7}{2}{x}^{7/2-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{5/2}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234299 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "21fbeb51-6dc3-543d-a41d-b167ecce3c56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{6}{x}^{-6/7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513623} {:answer/id #uuid "3a50a15f-c07f-5fc0-8f8a-c9858dca85ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513684} {:answer/id #uuid "47d643d4-e671-5e72-9cf0-0a89cbdb8d8b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{1/7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513726} {:answer/id #uuid "979719be-a5ae-506b-afac-4bb48471132a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{5/7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513925} {:answer/id #uuid "b3abc2ef-10c8-500f-8329-7a3eb25bffce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514001}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a50a15f-c07f-5fc0-8f8a-c9858dca85ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186513684} :answer-field/id #uuid "f072598f-e1c5-599b-affc-4413eaf94190" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186514344}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "44529618-fcf7-5581-8bd3-a0df68de242f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71100" :question/problem "Given that $f(x)={x}^{-1/7}$ and that ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x)$, calculate $F(x).$" :question/worked-solution "Remember that integration is the opposite of differentiation. So, if ${F}^{′}(x)=f(x),$ then integrating both sides of this equation gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(x) & =f(x) \\\\ \\int {F}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can use the power rule with $n=-\\frac{1}{7}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & =\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-1/7}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-1/7+1}}{(-\\frac{1}{7}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/7}}{\\left(\\frac{6}{7}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{6}{x}^{6/7}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234300 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb47b292-deba-58b8-a79e-0386e8ea55e3" :question/math-academy-id "q-71121" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int {x}^{10}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We apply the power rule with $n=10$, and we get\n\n$$\n\\int {x}^{10}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{10+1}}{10+1}+C=\\frac{{x}^{11}}{11}+C.\n$$\n\nLet's check by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{x}^{11}}{11}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{11}}{11}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{11}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{11})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{1}{11}\\cdot 11{x}^{10} \\\\ &= {x}^{10}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234301 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b23d50-ec7e-5283-bb24-57bc04c38817" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{4}{3{y}^{5}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516178} {:answer/id #uuid "590fc5e9-5d00-592b-afac-117c353a8649" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{y}^{5}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516301} {:answer/id #uuid "5c964151-5623-5fdc-9ce7-4cfbdba1065e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8}{{y}^{3}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516309} {:answer/id #uuid "6bff873d-db16-58c6-828d-9d04047ad325" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{2{y}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516348} {:answer/id #uuid "9efc680f-55f3-555d-96a2-ae3ddb7269d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8{y}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516436}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9efc680f-55f3-555d-96a2-ae3ddb7269d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{8{y}^{4}}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516436} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b2dd282-7c66-5a9f-93bd-d3e9bb5a8ef8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70a9bc2f-279b-5686-8f3f-46799ea34fe9" :question/math-academy-id "q-71163" :question/problem "Calculate $\\int \\frac{1}{2{y}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}y.$" :question/worked-solution "We can just take the constant factor out of the integral, and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{2{y}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int {y}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{y}^{-5+1}}{-5+1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{y}^{-4}}{-4}\\right)+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot {y}^{-4}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{8{y}^{4}}+C\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80a672a5-9018-5a78-9672-cf47be4d95f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}+3x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566608} {:answer/id #uuid "98e55f7b-e970-5ec1-8f87-371c4a6f55f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}-4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566663} {:answer/id #uuid "d87f3a3b-f1ff-5e22-81b2-679daf615e23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}-x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566796} {:answer/id #uuid "eb4e8d2e-8e7f-55f8-887c-4c3c6d134e97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{x}+4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566840} {:answer/id #uuid "f5261de7-027d-545e-bff9-4ea8c302891e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}+4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566855}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f5261de7-027d-545e-bff9-4ea8c302891e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}+4x+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186566855} :answer-field/id #uuid "f689fbb7-4820-549d-86af-2dc10f9a0677" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186567001}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79aa6724-ce36-5977-907b-506fab54c4d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-71169" :question/problem "Find the integral of $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}+4.$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}+4\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{x}^{3/2}}\\,\\text{d}x+4\\int 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int {x}^{-3/2}\\text{d}x+4\\int {x}^{0}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{-3/2+1}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}+1)}+4\\left(\\frac{{x}^{0+1}}{0+1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {x}^{-1/2}+4x+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{x}}+4x+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234303 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "09d58888-0116-53f3-b47a-9d0d7153229f" :question/math-academy-id "q-71173" :question/problem "$\\int 2+\\frac{1}{{y}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}y=$" :question/worked-solution "Applying the sum rule, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 2+\\frac{1}{{y}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\int 1\\,\\text{d}y+\\int {y}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\int {y}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y+\\int {y}^{-5}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =2\\left(\\frac{{y}^{0+1}}{0+1}\\right)+\\frac{{y}^{-5+1}}{-5+1}+C \\\\  & =2y+\\frac{{y}^{-4}}{-4}+C \\\\  & =2y-\\frac{1}{4{y}^{4}}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234342 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "058e8375-7ab8-5c53-aaed-f328087fbbae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339170} {:answer/id #uuid "13bbc739-722e-5acd-849e-2f17b762834c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339302} {:answer/id #uuid "45cfd739-1850-5ce4-b85f-97d485f27f1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339748} {:answer/id #uuid "67edeaf5-1d83-588c-b487-2b408b66d99c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡(x-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340055} {:answer/id #uuid "b1f89706-38a3-50fa-a7ed-dd30403522fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340748}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b1f89706-38a3-50fa-a7ed-dd30403522fa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340748} :answer-field/id #uuid "428de8a1-618a-5678-be18-aa4b392f0347" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341500}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "70fc9979-f75c-5347-868a-dff8c003766b" :question/math-academy-id "q-73178" :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that ${\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].$" :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{l})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{l}=a+lΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{\\pi }{2n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2n}=\\frac{(b-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right))}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{l}=-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n}$ and $f({x}_{l})={sec}^{2}⁡{x}_{l}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /4}{sec}^{2}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{l=0}^{n-1}[{sec}^{2}⁡(-\\frac{\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi l}{2n})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $f(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234343 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70017134-8714-5c98-afd4-4060bef0121a" :question/math-academy-id "q-73184" :question/problem "The area under the curve $y=\\operatorname{cos}(2x)$ between $x=0$ and $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ and the $x$-axis is approximated by a right Riemann sum using $5$ subintervals of equal length. What is the expression for the Riemann sum in sigma notation?" :question/worked-solution "The right Riemann sum for $y=f(x)$ with a regular step size in sigma notation is  \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx,\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{i}=a+iΔx$ with $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}.$ \n\nWe're told that there are $n=5$ subintervals. The endpoints indicate that $a=0$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$ So, the step size is\n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{\\frac{\\pi }{4}-0}{5}=\\frac{\\pi }{20}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using sigma notation, the right Riemann sum can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i})Δx & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}f(a+iΔx)Δx \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}f(0+i\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{20})\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{20} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}f\\left(\\frac{\\pi i}{20}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{20} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}\\frac{\\pi }{20}\\operatorname{cos}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi i}{20}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{20}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi i}{10}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234347 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4745bfc1-6a60-559c-95b5-8c1cc3217aef" :question/math-academy-id "q-73302" :question/problem "Express the following arithmetic series using sigma notation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{2}+5+\\frac{9}{2}+…+0\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The first term ${a}_{1}=\\frac{11}{2}$ and the common difference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =5-\\frac{11}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the formula for the $n$th term is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}+(n-1)\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}n+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{1}{2}n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe don't know the index of the last term, but we can find it using the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =0 \\\\ 6-\\frac{1}{2}n & =0 \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{1}{2}n \\\\ 12 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $12$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=12.$ So, the sigma notation for the given sum is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{12}(6-\\frac{1}{2}n).\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2209ff49-4e75-5f13-b1f8-712ee5777b5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "255" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339418} {:answer/id #uuid "9d2fb32d-3308-55e4-9dec-2aa683d7b600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "127" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340544} {:answer/id #uuid "9fe709df-a6a6-5133-a7ff-f182f177b723" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{255}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340575} {:answer/id #uuid "9ffac90e-c119-5243-85c0-ec200c7f7650" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340577} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f312b2-771e-58aa-86b8-bb472aa745e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{127}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340604}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d2fb32d-3308-55e4-9dec-2aa683d7b600" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "127" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340544} :answer-field/id #uuid "29a6af6c-2426-5c21-b15c-7c08dfb25fbb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341452}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "573548d5-0935-5420-8f7b-3bb41fa401b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-73306" :question/problem "The fifth term of a geometric series is $4$ and the sixth term is $2.$ What is the sum of the first $7$ terms?" :question/worked-solution "The sum of the first $N$ terms of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{1}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term of the series, and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nTo find $r,$ we find the ratio between the consecutive terms ${a}_{5}$ and ${a}_{6}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\frac{{a}_{6}}{{a}_{5}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow we must determine the value of ${a}_{1}.$ To do this, we use the formula for the $n$th term, which states that\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{n-1}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=5,$ we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{5-1} \\\\ 4 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{4} \\\\ 4 & ={a}_{1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{16} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =\\frac{4}{\\left(\\frac{1}{16}\\right)} \\\\ {a}_{1} & =64\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, if we substitute ${a}_{1}=64,N=7$ and $r=\\frac{1}{2}$ into the formula for ${S}_{N},$ we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{7} & =64\\cdot \\frac{1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{7}}{1-\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =64\\cdot \\frac{1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{7}}{\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{64}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}\\cdot (1-\\frac{1}{128}) \\\\  & =128(1-\\frac{1}{128}) \\\\  & =128-1 \\\\  & =127\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234353 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "76a94651-4a69-58ac-b4ae-9a0d42cb1364" :question/math-academy-id "q-73502" :question/problem "Calculate ${f}^{'}(x)$ for $f(x)=5\\operatorname{arccos}({e}^{x}).$" :question/worked-solution "The formula for the derivative of arccosine is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{arccos}x)=-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the chain rule and the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}[5\\operatorname{arccos}({e}^{x})] \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{({e}^{x})}^{2}}}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{({e}^{x})}^{2}}}\\right)\\cdot {e}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5{e}^{x}}{\\sqrt{1-{e}^{2x}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23b3ff8f-fef3-5d4b-8ac7-c7f0be3c20e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}{2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339438} {:answer/id #uuid "36b29201-973e-54a1-9076-d11d6f4d33f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186339618} {:answer/id #uuid "714604c9-2b37-531a-bb67-a0c67b2a5cb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340134} {:answer/id #uuid "d56615b3-79b5-569f-a38a-6eae7ea1c379" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341017} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc1530c-4d98-5329-89c2-53ade6de9cab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "714604c9-2b37-531a-bb67-a0c67b2a5cb6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186340134} :answer-field/id #uuid "9441441f-4088-5ff2-aa37-6b852eabed90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186341641}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9aadcdf1-9134-541b-ab5d-b526e490dc48" :question/math-academy-id "q-73670" :question/problem "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}\\,\\text{d}z.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's introduce the new variable $u={2}^{z}-9.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}z}={2}^{z}\\operatorname{ln}⁡2\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\,\\text{d}u={2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{2}^{z}}{\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}\\,\\text{d}z & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}\\cdot {2}^{z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{u}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\int {u}^{-1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\cdot \\frac{{u}^{1/2}}{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{{2}^{z}-9}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+C.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234365 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e8d8ebcd-fa6b-5ccc-84a2-1b62ba9761e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-74710" :question/problem "Determine the fourth partial sum of the series\n${\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let ${a}_{n}={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{n}.$ We note that this is a geometric sequence with $r=\\frac{1}{3}$ and first term given by\n\n$$\n{a}_{1}={\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{1}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nThe $k$th partial sum of a geometric series is given by\n\n$$\n{s}_{k}={a}_{1}\\left(\\frac{1-{r}^{k}}{1-r}\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the fourth partial sum is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{s}_{4}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{r}^{4})}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{4})}{(1-\\frac{1}{3})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{(1-\\frac{1}{81})}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}\\cdot \\frac{80}{81} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{80}{81}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{40}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234366 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eeb31b97-38d7-52f0-afc3-915c86bbc2a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-74726" :question/problem "Find, in radians, the argument of $z=5-5\\text{i}.$ \n\n**Hint**: The argument of a complex number is in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot $z$ in the complex plane.\n\n![](images/90/907a7699015953e2d57bb033d25f3bd3bb464f1b6c74fc5dd7cc0fc741942164.png)\n\nThe complex number $z$ lies in the fourth quadrant of the complex plane. Therefore $\\frac{3\\pi }{2}<\\operatorname{arg}(z)<2\\pi .$ \n\nIf $\\alpha $ is the angle that $z$ makes with the positive real axis, then\n\n$$\n\\alpha =\\operatorname{arctan}\\left(\\frac{5}{5}\\right)=\\operatorname{arctan}(1)=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arg}(z)=2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4}=\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234368 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "21cf0b12-845b-524d-bc78-3bc9f234746f" :question/math-academy-id "q-75001" :question/problem "Find $|r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)|$ given that $r(t)=⟨2\\operatorname{cos}t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}t,\\,6⟩.$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating the function at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right) & =⟨2\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),\\,4\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right),\\,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2},\\,4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\,6⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,2\\sqrt{3},\\,6⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|r\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\right)| & =\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(2\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+(6)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+12+36} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{49} \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "47ace125-1428-5370-906c-bbbbca66287f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "74" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527447} {:answer/id #uuid "7c5b7c2e-42ac-5c76-8d0e-167934a4ded1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527542} {:answer/id #uuid "83a8233c-86f1-5b7d-9702-f1ccf8ac79b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "63" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527562} {:answer/id #uuid "8a795631-2118-587a-9c52-e7fcae53626a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527580} {:answer/id #uuid "fbdf9a26-5fa5-5299-b0b5-3847f5e53bf1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527794}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a795631-2118-587a-9c52-e7fcae53626a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "64" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527580} :answer-field/id #uuid "7e7684e4-e06c-57a4-94c9-8c4065eb02cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527858}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02b30695-cbca-5de7-a2d0-90b3f98db6af" :question/math-academy-id "q-75637" :question/problem "Consider the positive infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $56$ and a third term equal to $\\frac{7}{8}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=56$ and ${a}_{3}=\\frac{7}{8}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{3-1} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)}{56} \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{56}\\right) \\\\ {r}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{64} \\\\ r & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{64}} \\\\ r & =\\frac{1}{8}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we have taken the positive square root since we're told that the sequence is positive.\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{(1-\\frac{1}{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)} \\\\  & =56\\left(\\frac{8}{7}\\right) \\\\  & =64.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234374 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74e312e6-4bb0-59a9-9a43-d2486edb08f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-75638" :question/problem "Consider the positive infinite geometric sequence that has a first term equal to $75$ and a fourth term equal to $\\frac{24}{5}.$ What is the sum to infinity of the terms of this sequence?" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFrom the question statement we have ${a}_{1}=75$ and ${a}_{4}=\\frac{24}{5}.$ We can find the common ratio using the following formula:\n\n$$\n{r}^{n-m}=\\frac{{a}_{n}}{{a}_{m}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in the known information into the above gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{4-1} & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{24}{5}\\right)}{75} \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\left(\\frac{24}{5}\\right)\\left(\\frac{1}{75}\\right) \\\\ {r}^{3} & =\\frac{8}{125} \\\\ {r}^{3} & ={\\left(\\frac{2}{5}\\right)}^{3} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{75}{(1-\\frac{2}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{75}{\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =75\\left(\\frac{5}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =125.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234378 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "22392e5f-342b-529d-86fd-ea844ee51752" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186342521} {:answer/id #uuid "736f476d-acc8-56c1-87cb-88c22b727e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343236} {:answer/id #uuid "8e4e2920-a601-564d-9948-09c988ca8a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343485} {:answer/id #uuid "ed30a6e1-9613-5cb9-a5e1-6b54f32d8a0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344324} {:answer/id #uuid "f35036d7-3881-5338-b9ba-68d962280411" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344378}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8e4e2920-a601-564d-9948-09c988ca8a03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186343485} :answer-field/id #uuid "352d0a1e-7a24-5d5d-a374-5deb9f4cf541" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186344585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1de086f6-6ce4-5e9d-90e8-e54489a7e2a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-75961" :question/problem "Which value of $y$ satisfies the equation $\\frac{y+1}{{y}^{2}}=\\frac{3}{2y}+\\frac{2y-1}{{y}^{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "We note that $y=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators ${y}^{2}$ and $2y.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}({y}^{2},2y)=2{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2{y}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y+1}{{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{3}{2y}+\\frac{2y-1}{{y}^{2}} \\\\ 2{y}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{y+1}{{y}^{2}}\\right) & =2{y}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2y}+\\frac{2y-1}{{y}^{2}}) \\\\ 2{y}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{y+1}{{y}^{2}}\\right) & =2{y}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{2y}\\right)+2{y}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2y-1}{{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\ 2\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{y+1}{{y}^{2}}\\right) & =y\\cdot 2y\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{2y}\\right)+2\\cdot {y}^{2}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2y-1}{{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\ 2\\cdot \\cancel{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{y+1}{\\cancel{{y}^{2}}}\\right) & =y\\cdot \\cancel{2y}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{\\cancel{2y}}\\right)+2\\cdot \\cancel{{y}^{2}}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{2y-1}{\\cancel{{y}^{2}}}\\right) \\\\ 2(y+1) & =3y+2(2y-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $y,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(y+1) & =3y+2(2y-1) \\\\ 2y+2 & =7y-2 \\\\ 7y-2y & =2+2 \\\\ 5y & =4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $y=0$ as a solution. But since $y=\\frac{4}{5}\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $y=\\frac{4}{5}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234379 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "43858207-8831-5183-b09f-a3da1530cacd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{9}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486740} {:answer/id #uuid "59d574d6-2cda-524f-ba83-6266f545c2b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486791} {:answer/id #uuid "64c98e51-6b85-5d4c-ac0f-82782d6de80e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486815} {:answer/id #uuid "a8e519cc-a7bd-5d74-8524-f56366c5b993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486975} {:answer/id #uuid "c27521f8-15fb-507d-92a2-e35438e66960" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487022}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a8e519cc-a7bd-5d74-8524-f56366c5b993" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486975} :answer-field/id #uuid "9c2d58ce-95e5-572a-9ac2-e040031c4dfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487226}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "10dcb0f4-5f0f-5b93-a598-6c0cd2ed4435" :question/math-academy-id "q-76105" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{3x+1}{2x-1}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{′}(2),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(2).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(2)=x.$ For $x\\ne \\frac{1}{2},$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(2) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{2x-1} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{2x-1}\\cdot (2x-1) & =2\\cdot (2x-1) \\\\ \\frac{3x+1}{\\cancel{(2x-1)}}\\cdot \\cancel{(2x-1)} & =4x-2 \\\\ 3x+1 & =4x-2 \\\\ 1+2 & =4x-3x \\\\ x & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{-1}(2)=3.$\n\nNow we calculate ${f}^{′}(3),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3x+1}{2x-1} \\\\ {f}^{′}(x) & =-\\frac{5}{(2x-1)^{2}} \\\\ {f}^{′}(3) & =-\\frac{5}{{(2\\cdot 3-1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{5}{{(5)}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}({f}^{-1}(2))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{′}(3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234380 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5192c69f-4919-5288-bf42-2e9b36903c79" :question/math-academy-id "q-76113" :question/problem "Consider the function $f(x)=2x+\\sqrt{x}.$ Given that $y=f(x)$ is invertible throughout its domain, determine $({f}^{-1})^{'}(1).$" :question/worked-solution "To determine $({f}^{-1})^{'}(1),$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n{({f}^{-1})}^{'}(1)=\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(1))}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the value of ${f}^{-1}(1).$ To do this, we solve the equation ${f}^{-1}(1)=x.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{-1}(1) & =x \\\\ f(x) & =1 \\\\ 2x+\\sqrt{x} & =1 \\\\ \\sqrt{x} & =1-2x \\\\ x & =1-4x+4{x}^{2} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}-5x+1 & =0 \\\\ (4x-1)(x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{4},\\,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nChecking the solutions, we notice that only $x=\\frac{1}{4}$ satisfies the equation $2x+\\sqrt{x}=1.$ Therefore, we discard $x=1$ and conclude that ${f}^{-1}(1)=\\frac{1}{4}.$\n\nNow, we calculate ${f}^{'}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =2x+\\sqrt{x} \\\\ {f}^{'}(x) & =2+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}} \\\\ {f}^{'}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) & =2+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)}} \\\\ {f}^{'}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({f}^{-1})}^{'}(1) & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}({f}^{-1}(1))} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{f}^{'}\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234423 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "beb183db-116e-587a-86b4-57c4c6938307" :question/math-academy-id "q-81074" :question/problem "Given that  ${a}_{n}=\\frac{5}{(n+1)^{2}}$ for $n\\ge 1,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. - $\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1$\nII. - ${a}_{n}$ is an increasing sequence\nIII. - ${a}_{n}$ is a decreasing sequence" :question/worked-solution "Suppose the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is a positive sequence defined for $n\\ge 1,$ and that\n\n$$\nL=\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}.\n$$\n\nThen,\n\n- if $L\\ge 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *increasing,* and\n\n- if $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ then ${a}_{n}$ is *decreasing.*\n\nNotice that, in this case, ${a}_{n}=\\frac{5}{(n+1)^{2}}$ is positive for all $n\\ge 1.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's analyze each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. Computing the ratio $L,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{5}{(n+2)^{2}}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{5}{(n+1)^{2}}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{5(n+1)^{2}}{5(n+2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(n+1)^{2}}{(n+2)^{2}} \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{n+1}{n+2}\\right)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $n\\ge 1,$ it follows that\n\n$$\n\\frac{n+1}{n+2}<1,\n$$\n\nsince the numerator is always smaller than the denominator. Therefore,\n\n$$\n0<L<1.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, whereas statement III is true. Since $0<L\\le 1$ for all $n\\ge 1,$ we can conclude that the sequence ${a}_{n}$ is decreasing.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1acd1b2a-5a27-52eb-af49-51c2d3f270e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348674} {:answer/id #uuid "39fdea12-56a2-5a31-b575-cf2f25d683b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348969} {:answer/id #uuid "3ef10c48-0f9d-5120-8f85-3e4161b23e98" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349020} {:answer/id #uuid "6a507b23-12f3-5642-bdee-bf77c04b34ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{30}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349427} {:answer/id #uuid "f1f911ff-f73b-56e3-a39a-9e61cd29e6ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{60}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350563}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1acd1b2a-5a27-52eb-af49-51c2d3f270e4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-{45}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348674} :answer-field/id #uuid "6de92a1d-25e7-5631-a857-03a6904516e5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350890}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5576ce75-f8bd-5322-bc04-5293079b93b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-82178" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](images/d9/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${315}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${315}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right)=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234463 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8aebb713-49e4-5555-96f2-5cd5aedf48e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-85520" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{tan}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right) & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)}{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234464 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "40e4c541-c196-5d3b-b771-296537e4001e" :question/math-academy-id "q-85524" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cot}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{cot}\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\theta }{\\operatorname{sin}\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cot}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)}{\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{3}\\right)}=\\frac{\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}{\\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\right)}=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234470 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e08de2a-a2d3-5bb5-9eff-7650cc85dd48" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512320} {:answer/id #uuid "95a56734-4623-5a29-823a-2dcc5eb112ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512451} {:answer/id #uuid "a3148c07-b30e-5ef9-a7ce-98aeae65443e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512480} {:answer/id #uuid "dd724d2d-9deb-58d6-b8f5-0793d0b3ea1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512631} {:answer/id #uuid "e559e8d5-044b-536f-be6b-c537f919870e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512647}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3148c07-b30e-5ef9-a7ce-98aeae65443e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512480} :answer-field/id #uuid "da5f6c2e-8aef-51f4-af0b-4f5580c1b351" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512844}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7f70c750-2bdb-5272-875c-7d304eb180d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-88472" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}=3{e}^{5x}-4x{e}^{5x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x} & =3{e}^{5x}-4x{e}^{5x} \\\\ 4{x}^{2}{e}^{5x}+4x{e}^{5x}-3{e}^{5x} & =0 \\\\ (4{x}^{2}+4x-3){e}^{5x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n4{x}^{2}+4x-3=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{e}^{5x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $4{x}^{2}+4x-3=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x+3)(2x-1)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The equation ${e}^{5x}=0$ has no solutions because ${e}^{5x}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\frac{3}{2},\\frac{1}{2},$ and their product is $\\left(-\\frac{3}{2}\\right)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}=-\\frac{3}{4}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234480 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "794aba26-caea-5909-8b60-b04c902155c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579170} {:answer/id #uuid "a129b0a1-c2ed-5249-bd0d-5716e80d81fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579246} {:answer/id #uuid "a42aadbd-d9a4-552b-b9c3-bc98e98e5a8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579251} {:answer/id #uuid "e494ab0a-1fba-527c-935d-7582c0252b56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579344} {:answer/id #uuid "f484c6f3-4a2f-533b-9895-d06159affa62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a129b0a1-c2ed-5249-bd0d-5716e80d81fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579246} :answer-field/id #uuid "e2e6b6da-b3b4-5e8f-8a0f-51c536b2805c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186579478}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "08d463f3-e7cd-52a1-b93d-d4bb3c2b2d27" :question/math-academy-id "q-92985" :question/problem "Find the equation of the horizontal asymptote of the function $f(x)={\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}^{x-3}+4.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=f(x):$\n\n![](images/47/47d570c5b05096a0e8b7a13df510d8cd71dbee93dd2bf58dae18d7ff49ff1787.png)\n\nSince the function approaches the line $y=4$ as $x\\to \\infty ,$ we conclude that the equation of the horizontal asymptote is $y=4.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234507 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2e9ec61c-a65c-56b3-820e-2d60ba70912c" :question/math-academy-id "q-9908" :question/problem "If a curve $C$ is given by $y=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}x}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x},$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, the expression for $y$ is a quotient $\\frac{u(x)}{v(x)}$ with $u(x)=\\operatorname{cos}x$ and $v(x)=1+\\operatorname{sin}x.$ \n\nTherefore, we apply the quotient rule as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{'}(x)\\cdot v(x)-u(x)\\cdot {v}^{'}(x)}{[v(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}x)^{'}\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{sin}x)-\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{'}}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x)(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)-(\\operatorname{cos}x)\\operatorname{cos}x}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-{sin}^{2}x-{cos}^{2}x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-({sin}^{2}x+{cos}^{2}x))}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Since ${sin}^{2}x+{cos}^{2}x=1$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{(-\\operatorname{sin}x-1)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)}{(1+\\operatorname{sin}x)^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{sin}x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) & =-\\frac{1}{1+\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1+1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $-\\frac{1}{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234508 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d03866e6-9a0b-5659-acf4-038e7cb18b72" :question/math-academy-id "q-99273" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "The graph $y={x}^{6}$ looks similar to an upward parabola, so\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }{x}^{6}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHowever, multiplying by the negative coefficient $-\\frac{1}{6}$ causes $y=-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}$ to look more like a downward parabola. This changes the sign of the limit. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }\\left(-\\frac{{x}^{6}}{6}\\right)=-{lim}_{x\\to -\\infty }({x}^{6})=-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234509 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0550e664-f03a-58d5-a906-a1b13b8d0a7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-99333" :question/problem "${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{9}}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the limit using the algebra of limits,  as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(-\\frac{6}{{x}^{9}}\\right) & =-6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, recall the fact that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}}\\right)=-\\infty ,\\,{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}}\\right)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nSo, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}}\\right)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n-6\\cdot {lim}_{x\\to 0}\\left(\\frac{1}{{x}^{9}}\\right)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234519 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7410dd72-8efd-59ee-929b-21eea426aeb9" :question/math-academy-id "q-99720" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{log}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}}\\right)=$" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite $\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}}$ as a power of $10$ (the base of the common logarithm) as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}}=\\frac{1}{{10}^{1/4}}={10}^{-1/4}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{10}}\\right)=\\operatorname{log}({10}^{-1/4})\n$$\n\nThe common logarithm reverses the action of the exponent. The principle is:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{x})=x\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have $x=-\\frac{1}{4}.$ Therefore:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{log}({10}^{-1/4})=-\\frac{1}{4}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234521 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b542da90-fa2b-563c-b1f0-c4fabe8e0315" :question/math-academy-id "q-9983" :question/problem "Calculate the sum to infinity of the geometric series $10-2+\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{25}+⋯$" :question/worked-solution "The sum to infinity ${S}_{\\infty }$ of a geometric series is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{\\infty }=\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r},\\,|r|<1,\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term and $r$ is the common ratio.\n\nFor the given geometric series, the first term is ${a}_{1}=10$ and the common ratio is\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{-2}{10}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, using the formula, we find that the sum to infinity is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{\\infty } & =\\frac{{a}_{1}}{1-r} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(1-\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right))} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{(1+\\frac{1}{5})} \\\\  & =\\frac{10}{\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)} \\\\  & =\\frac{50}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234523 :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "65ea4be7-7f1d-5940-92eb-a671bb136b70" :question/math-academy-id "q-99942" :question/problem "Calculate ${lim}_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{csc}(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=0$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to 0}\\operatorname{csc}(x+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\operatorname{csc}(0+\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{csc}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235347 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72a1ef1b-283f-5020-ba2d-b2f3346f1499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72a1ef1b-283f-5020-ba2d-b2f3346f1499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234720} :answer-field/id #uuid "79216e8c-2905-5a27-aa0b-652b2135680c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235120}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "df7cd8fe-37e2-547f-a82b-4acd638be632" :question/math-academy-id "q-128187" :question/problem "Use the tangent line approximation of $y(x)$ found in part 1 to estimate the value of $y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Our tangent line approximation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L(x) & =4x-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can approximate $y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ by evaluating the tangent line approximation at $x=\\frac{3}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) & ≈L\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-2 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235348 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80e7ba2c-f3da-5b13-acde-c1c4c8d12015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80e7ba2c-f3da-5b13-acde-c1c4c8d12015" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234743} :answer-field/id #uuid "51c3915d-9f67-5752-a50a-815c6237e44c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235058} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "82f69d44-046d-53d4-b5c4-befed46876e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234744}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "82f69d44-046d-53d4-b5c4-befed46876e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234744} :answer-field/id #uuid "657ffe4f-24a7-5925-8cc2-cadb10650d2f" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235078} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d3eecfa1-cc73-5709-b7b5-4b5d14838f23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d3eecfa1-cc73-5709-b7b5-4b5d14838f23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234877} :answer-field/id #uuid "6df74c9f-f142-51ad-ac12-61be12433bce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235095}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e503619d-21af-5f4b-b6eb-d5c17fa54595" :question/math-academy-id "q-128188" :question/problem "We wish to improve the approximation to $y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ found in step $2.$ To do this, we create a table and use Euler's method with step size $Δx=\\frac{1}{4}.$ The first row of our table is shown below. Find the missing values.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $a$ | $b$ | $c$ |\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $a=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $b=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $c=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, because the initial condition is $y(1)=2,$ we fill $y=2$ into the table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nNext, we compute ${y}^{′}$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{2} \\\\  & =1\\cdot {2}^{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $$ |\n\nFinally, we compute $Δy$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n\nTherefore, $a=2,$ $b=4,$ and $c=1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235349 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28991446-ab1e-5a00-8794-50fc9d328a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93/16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28991446-ab1e-5a00-8794-50fc9d328a5d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "93/16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234607} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b36cb32-3d96-57dd-a49e-b03269ddee44" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235016}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "17ab4fd5-a02b-55ea-bcd4-23bfc484ef65" :question/math-academy-id "q-128189" :question/problem "Use Euler's method to find an approximation to $y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ using two steps of equal size.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)≈$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll start with the table from part 3.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n\nWe now proceed to approximate $y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)$ using Euler's method.\n\n**Step 1.** We compute the values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=1+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{5}{4} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=2+1=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $3$ | $$ | $$ |\n\n**Step 2.** We compute ${y}^{′}$ and $Δy$ (using the values of $x$ and $y$ in the second row) and new values of ${x}_{\\text{new}}$ and ${y}_{\\text{new}}$ using Euler's method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′} & =x{y}^{2}=\\frac{5}{4}\\cdot {3}^{2}=\\frac{45}{4} \\\\ Δy & ={y}^{′}\\cdot Δx=\\frac{45}{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{45}{16} \\\\ {x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx=\\frac{5}{4}+\\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy=3+\\frac{45}{16}=\\frac{93}{16}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n| $\\,x\\,$ | $\\,y\\,$ | $\\,{y}^{′}\\,$ | $\\,Δy\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $1$ |\n| $\\frac{5}{4}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{45}{4}$ | $\\frac{45}{16}$ |\n| $\\frac{3}{2}$ | $\\frac{93}{16}$ | $$ | $$ |\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $y\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)≈\\frac{93}{16}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235356 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f8b9bdb-9838-55d0-bb49-abab662dbde1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\\left(\\frac{2}{27}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234769}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f8b9bdb-9838-55d0-bb49-abab662dbde1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}\\left(\\frac{2}{27}\\right)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234769} :answer-field/id #uuid "cc37ec3a-8597-5c61-a04b-d9684c51116f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235222}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6f9f3c49-fab7-5715-8827-fab0d52bd33d" :question/math-academy-id "q-128238" :question/problem "Find the value of ${\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Previously, we found that \n\n$$\n\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}=\\frac{5}{2(x+3)}-\\frac{3}{2(x+1)}.\n$$\n\nNow, we can integrate, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int (\\frac{5}{2(x+3)}-\\frac{3}{2(x+1)})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+3}\\,\\text{d}x-\\frac{3}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{x+1}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|-\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|+C & =\\frac{1}{2}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3|-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1|)+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+3{|}^{5}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x+1{|}^{3})+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{(x+3)^{5}}{(x+1)^{3}}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the definite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-5}^{-4}\\frac{x-2}{(x+3)(x+1)}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{(x+3)^{5}}{(x+1)^{3}}|]}_{-5}^{-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{{3}^{3}}\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{2}^{5}}{{4}^{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{4}^{3}}{{2}^{5}\\cdot {3}^{3}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{2}{27}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235371 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "71cce186-30b6-55d0-84d4-342f5c753f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234716}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "71cce186-30b6-55d0-84d4-342f5c753f10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234716} :answer-field/id #uuid "2ae52c6e-7b2f-5af5-8b96-5d84295d8e11" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235015} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f1e90155-6ac0-50f6-a4fb-8e3b6bbb20ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1e90155-6ac0-50f6-a4fb-8e3b6bbb20ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234928} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a6edf15-5a56-5db4-8849-32a5330ee399" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235030}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "333f44c6-e7aa-5f32-a7e4-9a58be270016" :question/math-academy-id "q-128339" :question/problem "Given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=A{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}+B{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants, find $A$ and $B.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $B=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the numerator and denominator of $f(x)$:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}=\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}\n$$\n\nOur task is to find $A$ and $B$ such that\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{A}{x-3}+\\frac{B}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nMultiplying both sides by $(x-3)(2x+1)$ gives\n\n$$\n7=A(2x+1)+B(x-3).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=-\\frac{1}{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(2\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+1)+B(-\\frac{1}{2}-3) \\\\ 7 & =-\\frac{7}{2}B \\\\ B & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=3,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =A(2\\cdot 3+1)+B(3-3) \\\\ 7 & =7A \\\\ A & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\frac{7}{(x-3)(2x+1)}=\\frac{1}{x-3}-\\frac{2}{2x+1}.\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }(\\frac{1}{x-3}-\\frac{2}{2x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $A=1$ and $B=-2.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235372 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e2f49d51-a569-54b6-8499-614594a99518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(9/2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234902}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e2f49d51-a569-54b6-8499-614594a99518" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(9/2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234902} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb66c969-a116-55b7-83c4-7bdad75642f0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235274}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "687b0f0d-b7fc-5c94-b602-200dc9ae2630" :question/math-academy-id "q-128340" :question/problem "${\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the indefinite integral first. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-3}{2x+1}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the improper integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-3}{2x+1}|]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{a-3}{2a+1}|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{9}|) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1-\\frac{3}{a}}{2+\\frac{1}{a}}|)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{9}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\right)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡9 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{9}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235373 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c0cffe24-94be-52e7-a238-f2cdd9eed192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234843}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c0cffe24-94be-52e7-a238-f2cdd9eed192" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234843} :answer-field/id #uuid "9adce06b-fd56-5896-83e4-f0d2d7a527b3" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235158}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "f8c9449b-c7f6-57bf-a86b-68a3efa54686" :question/math-academy-id "q-128660" :question/problem "Consider a function $g(x)$ such that $g(2)=-2$ and ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ If $h(x)=\\frac{5-25f(x)}{g(x)+3},$ then ${h}^{′}(2)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "In part 2 we found that ${f}^{′}(2)=-\\frac{21}{25}.$ Also,\n\n$$\nf(2)=\\frac{7}{2\\cdot {2}^{2}-5\\cdot 2-3}=-\\frac{7}{5}.\n$$\n\nThe quotient rule states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{u}{v}\\right)=\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nLet $u=5-25f(x)$ and $v=g(x)+3.$ Differentiating each, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=-25{f}^{′}(x)\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the quotient rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{{u}^{′}v-u{v}^{′}}{{v}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-25{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot (g(x)+3)-(5-25f(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x)}{(g(x)+3)^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt $x=2,$ this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(2) & =\\frac{-25{f}^{′}(2)\\cdot (g(2)+3)-(5-25f(2))\\cdot {g}^{′}(2)}{(g(2)+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-25\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{21}{25}\\right)\\cdot (-2+3)-(5-25\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{7}{5}\\right))\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}}{(-2+3)^{2}} \\\\  & =21-20 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235386 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8185b83-eb29-58df-bdeb-f269a4c2146c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6-12{t}^{2})/(2{t}^{3}+3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234829}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8185b83-eb29-58df-bdeb-f269a4c2146c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(6-12{t}^{2})/(2{t}^{3}+3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234829} :answer-field/id #uuid "719e6a79-1ce1-54b1-8c76-98d46b85de7a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "632a77f1-9681-5dcd-aa11-3a8387a03565" :question/math-academy-id "q-132978" :question/problem "The particle moves along the curve $y=2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x.$ Calculate ${y}^{′}(t)$ as a function of $t$ only.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${y}^{′}(t)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Differentiating the given relation with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{2}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we know that\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into our expression for ${y}^{′}(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}\\cdot 2{\\left(\\frac{2t}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}}\\right)}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{3}}\\cdot \\frac{2(2{t}^{2}+3)^{2}}{2t} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{t(2{t}^{2}+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235388 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bc58bfdf-5c61-564b-9179-b99d997e008a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{66}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234834}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bc58bfdf-5c61-564b-9179-b99d997e008a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{66}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234834} :answer-field/id #uuid "bbd67718-6c2e-5e11-a160-7539ce5a4737" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235206}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "76551198-3b30-5f90-a773-8f46f7e7ea21" :question/math-academy-id "q-132980" :question/problem "The acceleration of the particle in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ second is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "The particle's velocity in the $y$-direction is given by ${y}^{′}(t).$ Therefore, the acceleration in the $y$-direction is ${y}^{″}(t):$\n\nDifferentiating ${y}^{′}(t)$ using the quotient rule, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({y}^{′}(t)) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{6-12{t}^{2}}{2{t}^{3}+3t}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{(-24t)(2{t}^{3}+3t)-(6-12{t}^{2})(6{t}^{2}+3)}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-48{t}^{4}-72{t}^{2}-36{t}^{2}-18+72{t}^{4}+36{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{24{t}^{4}-72{t}^{2}-18}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(4{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2}-3)}{(2{t}^{3}+3t)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the acceleration at time $t=1,$ we substitute into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{″}(1) & =\\frac{6(4(1)^{4}-12(1)^{2}-3)}{(2(1)^{3}+3(1))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6(-11)}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{66}{25}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration in the $y$-direction when $t=1$ is $-\\frac{66}{25}\\,\\text{m}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235391 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28f0c4bf-c884-5ffe-9bbb-fd66e54891c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234608}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "28f0c4bf-c884-5ffe-9bbb-fd66e54891c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234608} :answer-field/id #uuid "c19367e3-97ce-5783-bf46-3e1a0dadc9a0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235212}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3c85a07f-1862-53e2-9986-87c4ee966897" :question/math-academy-id "q-132983" :question/problem "$\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t=\\operatorname{ln}⁡($\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$)+\\,K,$\nwhere $K$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "In part 9, we found that\n\n$$\n-16\\int \\frac{t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t=-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}+3)+K.\n$$\n\nUsing the sum and constant multiple rules, we compute the sum of the integrals of each function in the partial sum decomposition of ${y}^{′}(t)$ found in part 8:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {y}^{′}(t)\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int (\\frac{2}{t}-\\frac{16t}{2{t}^{2}+3})\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{2}{t}\\,\\text{d}t-16\\int \\frac{t}{2{t}^{2}+3}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡|t|-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{t}^{2}+3)+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡{t}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡((2{t}^{2}+3)^{4})+K \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{(2{t}^{2}+3)^{4}}\\right)+K.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235403 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d974e101-1852-5cc5-a2c2-e4157e1121a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234887}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d974e101-1852-5cc5-a2c2-e4157e1121a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234887} :answer-field/id #uuid "079df57d-3a7f-5abb-9ebe-062f08636271" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f4701d5c-31ce-52fa-8163-7497d5963cba" :question/math-academy-id "q-139445" :question/problem "Expressing the answer as a fraction, Maria's expected profit on her next turn is$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         dollars." :question/worked-solution "From part 5, we have the following probability mass function:\n\n| $f(X)$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ | $\\frac{1}{3}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X$ | $-6$ | $-2$ | $6$ |\n\nThe expected value of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & =-6\\cdot f(-6)+(-2)\\cdot f(-2)+6\\cdot f(6) \\\\  & =-6\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+(-2)\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)+6\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\  & =-2-\\frac{2}{3}+2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expected profit is $-\\frac{2}{3}$ dollars."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235404 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8af484f-e0a3-50cf-8367-274f5910242c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.137" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234912}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8af484f-e0a3-50cf-8367-274f5910242c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.137" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234912} :answer-field/id #uuid "ed6fe7ac-7dc7-519a-8260-f54be485f15a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0ffa4be7-fd0e-5296-b565-824e840e9738" :question/math-academy-id "q-139453" :question/problem "The money in the saving box accumulates until the very last game. Rounded to three decimal places, the probability that the saving box contains exactly $$20$ after $10$ games is$\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "In each drawn game, both players deposit $$2$ each, or $$4$ in total, into the saving box with probability $\\frac{1}{3}.$ \n\nHence, the saving box will contain $$20$ dollars if Maria and John draw $5$ games.\n\nThen, if $Y$ is the number of times a game is drawn out of $10$ possible games, then \n\n$$\nY∼B(10,\\frac{1}{3}).\n$$\n\nSo, $Y$ has the probability mass function\n\n$$\nf(y)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{y}){\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{y}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{10-y}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the desired probability is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(Y=5) & =f(5) \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{10}{5}){\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{5}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{10-5} \\\\  & =252{\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)}^{5}{\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)}^{5} \\\\  & =0.137,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235421 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b7f35e2d-66b6-54fe-a062-977604208172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234827}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b7f35e2d-66b6-54fe-a062-977604208172" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234827} :answer-field/id #uuid "13d44031-6cdd-5336-a8be-754448adef36" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6fce5f29-50bd-5618-8615-2260116a577f" :question/math-academy-id "q-166287" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x)=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to \\,\\pi /4}5sin⁡(2x) \\\\  & =5sin⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot sin⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235423 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e8be44b9-b42d-52ce-90b8-6bab2b790332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +2h)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234913}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e8be44b9-b42d-52ce-90b8-6bab2b790332" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{h}\\operatorname{sin}(\\pi +2h)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234913} :answer-field/id #uuid "fff82516-d651-5957-98ab-d8adc8fad4dd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235283}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9588cc2-fb5a-5a22-93e8-5666e235065a" :question/math-academy-id "q-166289" :question/problem "What is the average rate of change of the function $f(x)$ over the interval $x\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h],$ where $h>0$ is a parameter? Express your answer in terms of $h.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{Δf}{Δx}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We have the function $f(x)=5sin⁡(2x).$ Notice that we are given the length $h$ of the interval. So, the average rate of change of $f$ over this interval is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h},\n$$\n\nwith $a=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Substituting into the formula and simplifying gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{Δf}{Δx} & =\\frac{f(a+h)-f(a)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{f(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h)-f\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5sin⁡(2(\\frac{\\pi }{2}+h))-5sin⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5sin⁡(\\pi +2h)-5sin⁡(\\pi )}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5sin⁡(\\pi +2h)-5(0)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5sin⁡(\\pi +2h)}{h} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{h}sin⁡(\\pi +2h).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235433 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16242856-ba0f-5959-96a9-cc84fceb8d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x)}{8{sin}^{2}x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234573}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16242856-ba0f-5959-96a9-cc84fceb8d9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}x-\\operatorname{sin}x)}{8{sin}^{2}x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234573} :answer-field/id #uuid "7da92529-d318-5dfa-bfa7-0df43fae7490" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235128}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c7fd239-c698-5700-aea8-d260b2cef38a" :question/math-academy-id "q-167328" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f}{h}\\right)\\,=${{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f}{h}\\right) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)-f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x)}{[h(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}}{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235438 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6387390a-abb2-5dfb-b224-6f303cebe5ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6387390a-abb2-5dfb-b224-6f303cebe5ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5+\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234698} :answer-field/id #uuid "03f33ffc-fcf9-5c5d-8bb8-d042e1b2ae97" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ab911cf6-6e60-568c-ba99-7878f0275992" :question/math-academy-id "q-167558" :question/problem "![](images/37/37ec9bf31a46129d10531f179f16f1f8bb42b88428652ec02e1022ba958b51d5.png)     \n                                \n                            \n\n   \n\n                            The function $h$ is part of the graph of the function $y=5-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}\\right).$ Find the exact value of $y$ when $x=10.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $y\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{10\\pi }{3}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{10\\pi }{3}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{10\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{4\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $y=5-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{4\\pi }{3}\\right)=5+\\sqrt{3}.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235444 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0647bb9-4d67-5b7d-a9c8-a4806d762874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234897}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0647bb9-4d67-5b7d-a9c8-a4806d762874" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234897} :answer-field/id #uuid "f4aa3c58-8fd9-5d31-988a-048384e9e06f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235265}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "cee13c2e-6226-5f1c-9ea2-cd4611314d87" :question/math-academy-id "q-167564" :question/problem "The antiderivative of $\\sqrt[5]{x}$ equals\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $+\\,\\text{ }C$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\sqrt[5]{x}={x}^{1/5}$, we can apply the power rule with $n=\\frac{1}{5}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{1/5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{{x}^{1/5+1}}{(\\frac{1}{5}+1)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{6/5}}{\\left(\\frac{6}{5}\\right)}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's check our answer by differentiating:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}+C) &= \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{5{x}^{6/5}}{6}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(C) \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{6}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{6/5})+0 \\\\ &= \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot \\frac{6}{5}{x}^{6/5-1} \\\\ &= {x}^{1/5}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235445 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b95d6c4-5d7b-5dc8-88d7-f9b4485ad8c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234590}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b95d6c4-5d7b-5dc8-88d7-f9b4485ad8c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234590} :answer-field/id #uuid "7988cab9-4497-5a8a-841c-22ed9c51d002" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235121}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d280c885-e199-544c-b66d-467329b4f2c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-167566" :question/problem "Find the velocity of the particle at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $v\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nSince the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\theta =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating $v(t)$ at $t=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}$ we have\n\n$$\n2{csc}^{2}⁡\\left(\\frac{7\\pi }{6}\\right)=2{(-2)}^{2}=8.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235449 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fe4d5118-310c-52c1-8792-82278a9c6243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234945}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fe4d5118-310c-52c1-8792-82278a9c6243" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234945} :answer-field/id #uuid "2030a9e3-3cc4-5c91-8889-5080c5638f5c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235002}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "bfb96854-7123-5e58-a1c9-faaa3c019dff" :question/math-academy-id "q-167570" :question/problem "Another particle has a velocity function given by ${v}_{2}(t)=t\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right).$ Evaluate the velocity of this particle at $t=\\frac{16\\pi }{3}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${v}_{2}\\left(\\frac{16\\pi }{3}\\right)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\text{m/s}$" :question/worked-solution "Evaluating the function ${v}_{2}(t)$ at $t=\\frac{16\\pi }{3}$, we get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\left(\\frac{16\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{16\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nFirst, we find an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{8\\pi }{3}$ that lies in the range $[0,2\\pi ).$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8\\pi }{3}-2\\pi  & =\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right).$\n\nLet's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](images/db/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}).$\n\nSince the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\right)=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n{v}_{2}\\left(\\frac{16\\pi }{3}\\right)=\\left(\\frac{16\\pi }{3}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{8\\pi }{3}\\right)=-\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235450 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "52d0fb2c-78da-56c6-8581-f4b20ffce30c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234675}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "52d0fb2c-78da-56c6-8581-f4b20ffce30c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234675} :answer-field/id #uuid "83d6963f-f877-507f-bf95-e4d5ac3e236d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235135}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e8491154-cb55-56d4-b408-9058d547cc94" :question/math-academy-id "q-167571" :question/problem "Find ${lim}_{t\\to 5\\pi /2}{v}_{2}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Substituting $t=\\frac{5\\pi }{2}$ directly into the limit, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{t\\to 5\\pi /2}{v}_{2}(t) & ={lim}_{t\\to 5\\pi /2}t\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\right)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{2}\\cdot \\left(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =-\\frac{5\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8d0654e0-d49d-534f-9b33-68e3e971d9e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(t/2)-t/2\\operatorname{sin}(t/2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8d0654e0-d49d-534f-9b33-68e3e971d9e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(t/2)-t/2\\operatorname{sin}(t/2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234758} :answer-field/id #uuid "bdafbc71-e376-54dc-999f-eac8f84227b6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3dd0c3db-6967-5a89-a656-a9043eef1f70" :question/math-academy-id "q-167572" :question/problem "Find the acceleration function ${a}_{2}(t)$ of the second particle.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${a}_{2}(t)\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "In part 3, we saw that the derivative of a particle's velocity with respect to time gives its acceleration.\n\nTo differentiate ${v}_{2}(t),$ we require the product rule. Let $g(t)=t$ and $h(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right).$ Then\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(t)=1,\\,{h}^{′}(t)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right).\n$$\n\nWe used the chain rule to calculate ${h}^{′}(t).$\n\nNow, applying the product rule and simplifying, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2}(t) & ={v}_{2}^{′}(t) \\\\  & ={g}^{′}(t)\\cdot h(t)+g(t)\\cdot {h}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(1)(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right))+(t)(-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)-\\frac{1}{2}t\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f42bdd68-d7ff-53db-b3b0-bdc3bed81956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2/x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234932}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f42bdd68-d7ff-53db-b3b0-bdc3bed81956" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2/x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234932} :answer-field/id #uuid "7b42b4d1-0b65-5db6-846b-bc92dccc887b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235123}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "7ced32af-bad9-5a14-a058-555b5adb8c62" :question/math-academy-id "q-167634" :question/problem "$\\int \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,C$" :question/worked-solution "We take the constant factor out of the integral and then apply the power rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =2\\int {x}^{-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{x}^{(-2+1)}}{-2+1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\left(\\frac{{x}^{-1}}{-1}\\right)+C \\\\  & =-2\\cdot {x}^{-1}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{x}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235464 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c8001348-dd53-534f-8d2b-dd7335396166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234860}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8001348-dd53-534f-8d2b-dd7335396166" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{x}^{2}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234860} :answer-field/id #uuid "6fd67b44-4d31-5a37-a044-5583e68fcdce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235097}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "fd371490-b54b-5976-bf71-68a2fedb858d" :question/math-academy-id "q-167651" :question/problem "Write down the integral that gives the surface area of the shell.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Surface area $\\,=4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We can find the surface area generated when a curve is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis using the formula\n\n$$\nS=\\int 2\\pi y\\,\\text{d}s.\n$$\n\nSince we're given a curve in the form $y=f(x),$ we have \n\n$$\n\\text{d}s=\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we will use\n\n$$\nS=2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe are told that the limits of integration are from ${x}_{1}=0$ to ${x}_{2}=1.$ \n\nComputing the derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2})=4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}S & =2\\pi {\\int }_{{x}_{1}}^{{x}_{2}}y\\sqrt{1+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}(2{x}^{2})\\sqrt{1+(4x)^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{2}\\sqrt{1+16{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235481 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32ae6381-27b6-5f93-8131-98453b145862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(x)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234625}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32ae6381-27b6-5f93-8131-98453b145862" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}(x)-\\operatorname{sin}(x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234625} :answer-field/id #uuid "90dbff55-8f3a-5c54-81ca-c8b7d5227587" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235146}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4ea7df7-6856-52ff-9d9c-29beb9f68c2e" :question/math-academy-id "q-167782" :question/problem "Calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f}{h}\\right).$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f}{h}\\right)=\\frac{e^x}{8\\sin^2(x)}\\bigl(${{field-1}}$\\bigr)$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x).$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{f}{h}\\right) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)-f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x)}{[h(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{′}}{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x))}{16{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))}{16{sin}^{2}⁡(x)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x))}{8{sin}^{2}⁡(x)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235501 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9d00ec8-e865-5151-9a4c-bad4652bfb11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9d00ec8-e865-5151-9a4c-bad4652bfb11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234805} :answer-field/id #uuid "079110b6-6c4b-5d0b-9a1e-a17faca83de8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f543150-38e4-557e-9ab8-57056b911cc9" :question/math-academy-id "q-167938" :question/problem "Construct and simplify an integral representing the arc length of this circle using polar coordinates.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                         $\\,\\,\\text{d}\\theta $" :question/worked-solution "The arc length $L$ of a polar curve $r=r(\\theta )$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{\\theta }_{1}}^{{\\theta }_{2}}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }\\right)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\n$$\n\nSince $r=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta $, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta }=-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{r}^{2}+{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+{(-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}6\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235502 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a00763ad-4e74-5366-b4cf-96d7c6960003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234789}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a00763ad-4e74-5366-b4cf-96d7c6960003" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{12}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234789} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb3200af-b225-5fa1-908f-fd20b7ba66ce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235221}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0c54e8fe-499b-5467-a47e-09bd98bc5054" :question/math-academy-id "q-167939" :question/problem "The average distance from the origin to a point on the circumference of the circle is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "Since $r(\\theta )$ represents the distance of each point on the circle from the origin, the average distance is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{\\text{ave}} & =\\frac{1}{[\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)]}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}6\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ){|}_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)-6\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\pi }(6-(-6)) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235514 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fa232cfc-645e-5d26-8f7a-e64c1ba82e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "109" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234941}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fa232cfc-645e-5d26-8f7a-e64c1ba82e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "109" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234941} :answer-field/id #uuid "232c8eb8-e105-5135-97a7-de91270cf6ef" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235006}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5608163c-bf57-5039-b653-5bdcdbba7103" :question/math-academy-id "q-168036" :question/problem "Find the angle $\\theta $ between vectors $q$ and $r.$ Round your answer to the nearest degree.\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\theta =$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "To find the angle $\\theta $ between the vectors, we can rearrange the dot product formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q\\cdot r & =|\\,q\\,|\\cdot |\\,r\\,|\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{q\\cdot r}{|\\,q\\,||\\,r\\,|}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom part 4, we have $|\\,q\\,|=\\sqrt{13}$ and $|\\,r\\,|=5.$ Also, from part 5, we have $q\\cdot r=-6.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  & =\\frac{q\\cdot r}{|\\,q\\,||\\,r\\,|} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-6)}{\\sqrt{13}\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =-\\frac{6}{5\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(-\\frac{6}{5\\sqrt{13}}\\right)≈{109}^{∘}.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235518 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f4b1b0a-72e5-51b7-976a-a11cf6167471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0009(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f4b1b0a-72e5-51b7-976a-a11cf6167471" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.0009(T-25)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186234768} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab8df629-31a4-5279-82d1-b3a16b07e686" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186235183}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a44df9e2-3cc1-515c-b385-288559e72721" :question/math-academy-id "q-168076" :question/problem "Find $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}$ in terms of $T.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}}\\,=\\,$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To find an expression for the second derivative, we differentiate the equation with respect to $t,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\right) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-0.03(T-25)) \\\\ \\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-0.03T+0.75) \\\\  & =-0.03\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(T)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0.75) \\\\  & =-0.03\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we substitute the given expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}$ into the above and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{2}T}{\\text{d}{t}^{2}} & =-0.03\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =-0.03(-0.03(T-25)) \\\\  & =0.0009(T-25)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]
